---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="How It Works | Credits That Count"
  description="How a Credits That Count plan is prepared and delivered, our make it right policy, and what we collect and never collect."
>
  <div class="container">
    <section class="block" style="margin-top:26px;">
      <h2>How delivery works</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          The family completes a short intake: current college, target
          college, courses taken or planned, and any AP scores.
        </li>
        <li>
          The system generates the check, the transfer degree plan, the
          admission standing, and the savings estimate from verified sources.
        </li>
        <li>
          The Signed Audit tier adds a Credit Transfer Pro's personal review
          of the generated plan against the published sources before
          delivery. Our Credit Transfer Pros are former and current higher
          education administrators with experience in college student
          affairs, admissions, and student success.
        </li>
        <li>
          Delivery is by email as a printable document, dated and edition
          stamped, with the source list attached.
        </li>
        <li>
          Turnaround is always 48 hours or less, for the plan and the signed
          audit alike.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>What this is</h2>
      <p>
        Credits That Count turns Louisiana's published transfer rules into a
        personal plan for one student. Every answer is built from official
        published sources: the Louisiana Board of Regents Course Articulation
        Matrix, the statewide common course catalog, college catalogs,
        published admission standards, and published tuition schedules, each
        dated and edition stamped on the plan itself.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is guidance, not an official ruling. Only the receiving college
        makes the final decision on how a credit applies, and requirements
        can change. A plan states what the published sources said on the date
        it was prepared, and names its sources so a family can verify every
        line with the college.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <div class="secgroup">
        <p class="secband">Our make it right policy</p>
        <p>
          If a plan or audit is wrong because our data was wrong, we fix it
          and refund it: the family receives a corrected plan at no charge
          and a full refund of what they paid for that product.
        </p>
        <p>
          If a college changed its requirements after the plan date, the
          corrected plan is issued at no charge and the plan's monitoring
          option covers the update.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="block">
      <h2>Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        We collect the minimum needed to prepare a plan: a parent name and
        email, the student's current college, target college, and the courses
        taken or planned. We do not ask for transcripts, Social Security
        numbers, or student account passwords. Family information is used
        only to prepare and deliver the product and is never sold.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="btn" href="/checker/">Start with the free checker</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="tagline-foot">Know before you go.</p>
  </div>
</Base>
